* Loops and branches with names on them.
*
* Every other program here writes its loops out: `@to counter` `[` ... `]`,
* with a `@to counter` at the end that must not be forgotten. A macro could
* not stand in for that, because a macro could take a cell and a count but not
* a *body*.
*
* It can now. `@while`, `@when` and `@unless` are ordinary macros in
* `lib/idioms.bfm`, and the body goes after the invocation in braces -- the
* same shape `@macro` itself uses to take one.
*
* What it does:
*
*     for n in (3, 0, 5):
*         if n:
*             print('[')
*         if not n:
*             print('-')
*         while n:
*             print('*')
*             n -= 1
*         print(' ')
*
* `@repeat` is the other half: a count settled when the program is built,
* around a body rather than around one instruction. `+{3}` has always been
* able to repeat a `+`; nothing could repeat a line.

@include "lib/idioms.bfm"

@var n
@var flag
@var scratch
@var spare
@var out

* Stars if there are any, a dash if there are not.
*
* Both questions are asked before the loop, because the loop is what empties
* the cell: asking afterwards would say every bar was empty, which is the
* first thing this program got wrong.
@macro bar(count) {
    @put(n, count)
    @when(n, flag, scratch, spare) {
        @say(out, '[')
    }
    @unless(n, flag, scratch, spare) {
        @say(out, '-')
    }
    @while(n) {
        @say(out, '*')
        @to n
        -
    }
    @say(out, SPACE)
}

@bar(3)
@bar(0)
@bar(5)
@newline(out)

* A count that is settled before the program runs.
@repeat 4 {
    @say(out, '=')
}
@newline(out)
